# ============================================================================
# Next major version — schema cleanup
# ============================================================================
# Drop transactions.user_id and scheduled_transactions.user_id. They are
# redundant with accounts.user_id (the account owner) and were retained in
# v1.15.0 only to keep many indexes/queries (idx_transactions_user_date,
# idx_transactions_user_sync, sync filters) working without a larger refactor.
# Real attribution lives in created_by_user_id.
#
# To do this safely:
#   1. Migrate every WHERE t.user_id = ? query to filter by account_id IN (...)
#      (most call sites already do this since v1.15.0 — audit the rest).
#   2. Update the Android sync layer to stop relying on user_id as a partition
#      key.
#   3. Drop the indexes that reference user_id, then drop the column.

# ============================================================================
# Scheduled-tx idempotency — done (v1.18.0) at the practical level
# ============================================================================
# v1.18.0 makes the scheduler write a deterministic client_id of the form
# "sched-<scheduled_id>-<next_occurrence>" on every materialization. The
# existing UNIQUE partial index on (created_by_user_id, client_id) rejects
# any re-fire as a UNIQUE constraint violation; the wrapping transaction
# rolls back, no balance double-count, no next_occurrence re-advance.
#
# A schema-level scheduled_transaction_id FK + UNIQUE(FK, date) partial
# index would be strictly stronger (lets the scheduled-tx UI derive
# "X/Y occurrences" from COUNT() instead of the brittle counter field).
# File as a fresh ticket if/when wanted.


# ============================================================================
# Recurrence: true RFC 5545 month semantics
# ============================================================================
# Since v1.22.0 / Android 1.33.0 both platforms agree on month-end rollover:
# MONTHLY/YEARLY clamp the day of month to the target month's last valid day
# (2026-01-31 + 1 month -> 2026-02-28). Go's addMonthsClamped was written to
# match java.time.LocalDate.plusMonths, which the Android worker already used.
#
# Neither implements true RFC 5545, which keeps the original day-of-month as
# an anchor and *skips* months that have no such day: Jan 31 -> Mar 31, never
# Feb 28. Our clamp is sticky instead, so a schedule that lands on Feb 28
# stays on the 28th forever.
#
# A durable fix needs an anchor day-of-month stored alongside the schedule
# (scheduled_transactions.anchor_day, defaulting to the day of the initial
# next_occurrence), advanced from the anchor rather than from the previous
# occurrence. Backend, Android worker and the form preview all have to change
# together, and existing rows need a backfill from their creation date.


# ============================================================================
# Android single-edit schedule form: stale next_occurrence on push
# ============================================================================
# ScheduledRepository.update queues a full-row UPDATE whose payload carries
# the next_occurrence Android last pulled. If the server scheduler advanced
# the schedule in the meantime, the PUT rewinds next_occurrence to an
# occurrence that was already materialized; processOne then collides on the
# deterministic "sched-<id>-<next_occurrence>" client_id, rolls back and never
# advances, so the schedule silently stops. Bulk edit (v1.27.0 / Android
# 1.39.0) avoids this with partial PATCH ops; the single-edit form still has
# it. Fix: omit next_occurrence from the UPDATE payload unless the user
# actually changed it (needs a server-side "keep current" semantics or a
# PATCH-style scheduled update endpoint).


# ============================================================================
# Pre-existing sync / data issues found by the v1.27.0 bulk-edit review
# ============================================================================
# Confirmed with probes; not caused by bulk edit, left for a dedicated fix.
#
# Android (SyncRepository push loop):
#  1. Child op sent with its parent's negative FK can never sync. Deferral only
#     looks at the op's own id; a transaction CREATE whose account CREATE failed
#     transiently goes out with account_id=-2, fails permanently (403), and the
#     remap FK rewrite only touches *pending* ops, so Retry resends -2 forever.
#     Fix: also defer/hold ops whose payload account_id/category_id/parent_id
#     is negative; let rewrite*Fk cover failed ops too.
#  2. failStuckDeferred marks every pending negative-id non-CREATE op failed,
#     including ops inserted during the drain whose CREATE was never tried.
#     Fix: only fail the ids actually deferred in that drain.
#  3. (fixed in 1.39.0: duplicate server rows after 401 → re-login → Retry;
#     one CREATE per row, 401 keeps ops pending, success drops other CREATEs)
#  4. AccountRepository/CategoryRepository create: id allocation + upsert + op
#     insert are not in one withTransaction (Transaction/Scheduled are).
#
# Backend:
#  5. Batch handlers use deferred transactions; under concurrent writers the
#     read→write upgrade fails fast with SQLITE_BUSY/BUSY_SNAPSHOT (500, no
#     corruption). Fix if it matters: BEGIN IMMEDIATE for write handlers.
#  6. handleDeleteCategory checks "no rows reference it" outside a transaction;
#     a concurrent assignment can leave rows on a soft-deleted category.
#  7. match=exact trims only ASCII spaces in SQL (TRIM) while q is trimmed with
#     strings.TrimSpace / Kotlin trim(): "Rent\t" is not an exact match.
#
# Web:
#  8. "Select all matching" pages by offset; a concurrent insert/delete on
#     another device between pages can skip a row (counts are reported).
#  9. Single-entry forms list every accessible owner's categories in "all
#     owners" mode; the server does not check category ownership on PUT/POST.
